  "body": "## What changed\n\nA STEP record nested about 1000 parentheses deep overflowed the stack inside ruststep's recursive-descent parser and killed the process (#1075). Before ruststep runs, a new linear byte pre-scan, `crates/app/src/geometry/import/step_depth.rs`, tracks parenthesis depth outside strings (`'...'`, with `''` as an escaped quote) and comments (`/* ... */`). Past `MAX_NESTING = 64` it refuses the file by name:\n\n`corrupt STEP: record nests deeper than 64 levels of parentheses at entity #999999, line 178` (the 5000-deep test input)\n\n- Single pass over the bytes, no copy, scans bytes rather than chars (non-UTF-8 is fine), and has no unwrap/expect/panic.\n- Unbalanced or negative depth, and unterminated strings or comments, are left to the parser.\n- Line numbers count `\\n` only, so CRLF and LF files give the same line.\n\n**Call sites (one line each, the first statement of the function):**\n- `step::parse_with_chord`\n- `step_csys::read_tool_csys`\n\n**Entry points covered:**\n- Every model import reaches `parse_with_chord` through `import::load_model[_graded]`: GUI `ImportModel`, including the worker thread and its inline fallback; `ImportFixture` and the fixture command (`commands/fixture.rs`); the tool-body mesh (`commands/tool_body.rs`, `tool_body_view.rs`); and MCP `import_model` and the script `import`, both through `script::api::v_import` → `ImportModel`.\n- The tool body's coordinate-system read hands bytes to ruststep separately through `read_tool_csys`, and is guarded too.\n- `UpdateModel` takes a mesh, not bytes, so it needs no guard.\n- Project load does not re-import from files: no call to the importers outside the paths above.\n- The kernel-side importer (`crates/kernel`, PR #941, not merged) caps nesting at 64 itself and is not touched.\n\n**Overlap with PR #1006:** both PRs touch `step::parse_with_chord`. #1006 edits the hunk around the `ruststep::parser::parse` error return. This PR adds one line at the top of the function, two lines above that hunk, so they should merge without conflict.\n\n## Review round 1 (review 5869429278): the scan now mirrors ruststep's tokenizer\n\n**What was wrong:** the scan knew only strings and comments. ruststep also reads `<` ... `>` (REFERENCE resources and ANCHOR names) as text. So `<x/*y>` or `<x'y>` put the scan into a comment or string state that ruststep never entered, and every `(` after it went uncounted.\n\n**What changed:**\n- The scan gained a `Uri` state for `<...>`.\n- It also gained a `Binary` state for `\"...\"`. ruststep cannot parse binary literals; inside one the scan still counts `(` and ignores `'`, `/*` and `<`.\n- I read ruststep's whole tokenizer at the pinned rev 90fdc93 (`token.rs`, `combinator.rs`, `exchange/*.rs`). The construct-by-construct table is in the module doc and in `docs/decisions/1075-step-nesting-cap.md`.\n- Where ruststep's rule is unclear, the scan counts the parenthesis.\n- The decision file and the comment beside `[patch.crates-io]` in the root `Cargo.toml` now say the scan must be re-audited whenever the ruststep pin moves. They also say that the move to solidcore (#917) removes the dependency.\n\n**New tests in `step_nesting.rs`:**\n- `a_reference_resource_cannot_hide_a_deep_record`: both of the reviewer's reproductions, at depth 65.\n- `no_lexical_construct_hides_a_deep_record`: 15 constructs, each with a stray `'`, `/*`, `<` or `\"` inside, followed by a record one past the cap. The scan must refuse each one.\n- `parentheses_inside_text_are_not_counted`: 200 `(` inside a string, a comment and a resource. The file still imports through the real parser.\n\n**Safety of the tests:** every deep input is checked by the scan before the importer sees it, so no test can hand ruststep a record deeper than the cap.\n\n**Mutation check:** I removed the `<` and `\"` states and re-ran `step_nesting`. The three new tests failed and the rest passed.\n\n## The cap: measured crash depths\n\nruststep at the pinned rev, a single record nested N deep, on Windows 11:\n\n| build | 1 MiB stack | 2 MiB stack | 8 MiB stack |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| debug, last N that survives | 400 | 821 | 3341 |\n| release, last N that survives | 530 | 1086 | 4418 |\n\n**Fixtures:** every `testdata/models/*.step` reaches a maximum depth of 3. That covers all nine files: box, empty_list, Milling-test, Milling-test-inch, sample_ap214 (a real AP214 export), and the four tool_csys files.\n\n**Why 64:**\n- The smallest default stack an import runs on is the 1 MiB Windows main thread, used by the GUI's inline fallback.\n- 64 is about 20x the deepest real record and about 6x below the debug crash on 1 MiB.\n- It matches the solidcore importer's cap.\n\nThe reasoning and the options dropped (a big-stack thread cannot catch an overflow) are in `docs/decisions/1075-step-nesting-cap.md`.\n\n## How tested\n\n**Pinning tests in `crates/app/tests/step_nesting.rs`** (new, default lane, 8 tests, 0.06 s total). The deep inputs are built in code from box.step; no fixture is committed. Every deep input goes through the scan before the importer sees it.\n- `a_record_nested_5000_deep_is_refused_by_name_quickly`: the message names the cap, `entity #999999` and the line, in under 1 s.\n- `crlf_and_lf_files_name_the_same_line`\n- `a_record_at_the_cap_still_imports`: depth 64 imports the same box mesh; depth 65 is refused.\n- `the_tool_body_csys_read_is_guarded_too`\n- `a_reference_resource_cannot_hide_a_deep_record`: the review's `<x/*y>` and `<x'y>` reproductions, at depth 65.\n- `no_lexical_construct_hides_a_deep_record`: 15 constructs, from the table, each followed by a record one past the cap.\n- `parentheses_inside_text_are_not_counted`: a file with 200 `(` inside a string, a comment and a resource still imports through ruststep.\n- `every_step_fixture_is_under_the_cap`\n\n**Lib tests in `step_depth::tests`** (7): strings, comments, `<...>` and binary literals; `''` escapes; refusal at the cap; CRLF; unbalanced input; non-UTF-8 input.\n\n**Gates, on head 45472061:**\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main`: green, 30.6 min. The comment added to the root `Cargo.toml` widened the quick gate to every crate: geometry, cl, tools, post, sim, motion, app, ui and clippy, 0 failed.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test step_nesting --test import --test tool_csys`: 8/8, 23/23 and 11/11 pass.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --lib step`: 18/18 pass.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The GUI was not run.\n- The crash depths were measured once with a throwaway probe test (not committed), on Windows only. Linux and macOS main threads are 8 MiB, so the cap leaves even more room there.\n- STL and OBJ are not recursive parsers and need no such guard. There is no DXF importer in the tree yet.\n\nChangelog: A STEP file with a record nested more than 64 parentheses deep is refused by name (entity and line) instead of crashing the app with a stack overflow.\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #1075\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n",
